  "vtt": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.280\nDavid and a business partner review what happens if one of\n\n2\n00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:08.100\nthem dies. If the company needs funds to replace the lost\n\n3\n00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.200\nservices of an essential worker, key-person coverage can address that\n\n4\n00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.320\nbusiness loss. The business generally owns the policy and receives the\n\n5\n00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:17.640\nbenefit under that arrangement.\n\n6\n00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:22.220\nA buy-sell agreement addresses a different problem: how an ownership interest\n\n7\n00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.520\nwill be purchased after a specified event.\n\n8\n00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.700\nLife insurance can fund the purchase obligation, but the agreement\n\n9\n00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:34.020\nestablishes the transaction. In a cross-purchase design, the owners typically\n\n10\n00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:39.320\ninsure one another; in an entity-purchase design, the business generally owns\n\n11\n00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.360\nthe policies. Do not assume a key-person payment automatically transfers the\n\n12\n00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:49.233\ndeceased owner's shares. David also has pretax retirement funds in a\n\n13\n00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:51.613\nformer employer's four-oh-one-k plan.\n\n14\n00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:56.633\nHe considers an eligible direct rollover into a traditional individual\n\n15\n00:00:56.633 --> 00:01:00.893\nretirement account. Direct payment goes to the eligible receiving arrangement\n\n16\n00:01:00.893 --> 00:01:03.773\nand avoids mandatory twenty percent withholding.\n\n17\n00:01:04.613 --> 00:01:08.653\nMoving eligible pretax funds into a Roth arrangement instead generally\n\n18\n00:01:08.653 --> 00:01:13.073\nintroduces conversion income. If an annuity is used inside the traditional\n\n19\n00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:16.633\naccount, the account already provides tax deferral.\n\n20\n00:01:17.193 --> 00:01:20.933\nThe annuity must be evaluated for its other actual features, charges\n\n21\n00:01:21.493 --> 00:01:25.853\nand restrictions. Do not describe an additional layer of tax deferral\n\n22\n00:01:25.853 --> 00:01:29.313\nas an automatic benefit of placing an annuity inside an already\n\n23\n00:01:29.313 --> 00:01:34.095\ntax-deferred retirement account. Rosa and David want reliable retirement\n\n24\n00:01:34.095 --> 00:01:38.895\nincome but also need access to money for near-term expenses and\n\n25\n00:01:38.895 --> 00:01:43.255\nemergencies. A recommendation that commits all their liquid resources to a\n\n26\n00:01:43.255 --> 00:01:47.535\nlong surrender schedule may conflict with those needs, even if its\n\n27\n00:01:47.535 --> 00:01:49.315\nillustrated income looks attractive.\n\n28\n00:01:50.315 --> 00:01:54.755\nReview the actual consumer profile, available alternatives, liquidity,\n\n29\n00:01:55.555 --> 00:02:00.295\ntime horizon, costs, surrender terms and the features supporting the\n\n30\n00:02:00.295 --> 00:02:05.095\nrecommendation. For a covered Maryland annuity recommendation, best interest\n\n31\n00:02:05.095 --> 00:02:10.775\nincludes the required care, disclosure, conflict and documentation duties.\n\n32\n00:02:11.855 --> 00:02:15.235\nAn agent's compensation cannot be the reason to put the consumer's\n\n33\n00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:19.635\ninterest second. The worksheet should explain why this specific arrangement\n\n34\n00:02:19.635 --> 00:02:24.975\nfits the known circumstances, including what resources remain available after\n\n35\n00:02:24.975 --> 00:02:29.227\nthe purchase. Compare two fictional income quotes with identical starting\n\n36\n00:02:29.227 --> 00:02:31.267\nfunds and no other features.\n\n37\n00:02:32.007 --> 00:02:36.467\nPure life pays nine hundred dollars monthly while Rosa lives and\n\n38\n00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:37.547\nstops at her death.\n\n39\n00:02:37.875 --> 00:02:41.432\nA life option with a ten-year certain period pays eight hundred\n\n40\n00:02:41.432 --> 00:02:46.602\nfifty dollars monthly, continuing for her life and guaranteeing the stated\n\n41\n00:02:46.602 --> 00:02:51.022\nminimum payment period. If she dies after exactly twenty-four monthly\n\n42\n00:02:51.022 --> 00:02:55.782\npayments under the second option, ninety-six guaranteed monthly payments\n\n43\n00:02:55.782 --> 00:02:59.202\nremain for the beneficiary under these assumptions.\n\n44\n00:03:00.142 --> 00:03:04.282\nIf she lives beyond ten years, her lifetime payments continue.\n\n45\n00:03:05.002 --> 00:03:09.122\nThe certain period is a minimum guarantee, not the end of\n\n46\n00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:14.282\nlifetime income. The quoted amounts are teaching assumptions; actual\n\n47\n00:03:14.282 --> 00:03:16.742\nrates depend on the contract and its pricing.\n\n48\n00:03:17.562 --> 00:03:21.922\nNow consider David's separate personally owned nonqualified annuity issued in\n\n49\n00:03:21.922 --> 00:03:25.856\n2020. It has a ninety thousand dollar value and sixty thousand\n\n50\n00:03:25.856 --> 00:03:30.282\ndollars of adjusted investment, with no earlier withdrawals, debt or other\n\n51\n00:03:30.282 --> 00:03:34.629\nadjustments. Before annuitization, he takes an ordinary twenty thousand\n\n52\n00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:38.682\ndollar partial withdrawal. The thirty thousand dollars of gain is reached\n\n53\n00:03:38.682 --> 00:03:42.842\nfirst, so the full twenty thousand withdrawn is taxable income under\n\n54\n00:03:42.842 --> 00:03:47.902\nthese facts. David is sixty-four, so the ordinary age-based ten percent\n\n55\n00:03:47.902 --> 00:03:51.062\nadditional tax does not apply, but the income tax remains.\n\n56\n00:03:51.882 --> 00:03:55.042\nThis is different from a properly qualifying exchange of the annuity\n\n57\n00:03:55.042 --> 00:03:59.662\ninto another annuity. That exchange can defer current gain recognition while\n\n58\n00:03:59.662 --> 00:04:03.002\ncarrying forward the relevant investment, rather than creating fresh\n\n59\n00:04:03.002 --> 00:04:05.042\nafter-tax basis at current value.\n\n60\n00:04:05.625 --> 00:04:10.305\nAcross these cases, begin by identifying the person or business being\n\n61\n00:04:10.305 --> 00:04:12.745\nprotected and the event that causes payment.\n\n62\n00:04:13.465 --> 00:04:17.385\nNext identify the legal arrangement, who owns it, who receives the\n\n63\n00:04:17.385 --> 00:04:20.305\nbenefit and which money is pretax or after-tax.\n\n64\n00:04:21.145 --> 00:04:25.785\nThen apply the actual time limit, contract provision or Maryland duty\n\n65\n00:04:25.785 --> 00:04:27.485\nbefore doing the arithmetic.\n\n66\n00:04:28.765 --> 00:04:32.425\nRead the question's assumptions instead of carrying a rule from a\n\n67\n00:04:32.425 --> 00:04:34.365\ndifferent product into the scenario.\n\n68\n00:04:35.625 --> 00:04:39.765\nDuring timed practice, flag difficult questions and return with the remaining\n\n69\n00:04:39.765 --> 00:04:43.185\ntime. Then review missed answers and the linked lessons.\n\n70\n00:04:44.125 --> 00:04:47.885\nCourse completion records study progress; applying knowledge to fresh\n\n71\n00:04:47.885 --> 00:04:50.125\nquestions tests understanding.\n\n",
